course: dictionary
lesson: 2524
title: Decoding: turning letters into words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Decoding means turning a secret code or message back into normal words so it can be read. In reading, it means sounding out the letters to work out what a written word says. Blend the sounds together: c-a-t, cat.
explain: «Decoding (turning letters into words) and comprehension (understanding what you read) are two different skills.» The brackets explain each one. Decoding gets you from the letters to the word. Comprehension gets you from the word to its meaning.
explain: You can decode a word perfectly and still not understand it. If you read a word aloud but do not know what it means, use the words around it as context clues, just as you would with any new word.
fact: Decoding means turning letters into words, usually by sounding them out.
fact: Decoding can also mean turning a secret code back into normal words.
fact: In computing, decoding means changing data into a form that people or machines can use, like turning a file into a video.
fact: «Decoding» can be a verb (we are decoding the message) or a noun (decoding is a key reading skill).
fact: Decoding and comprehension are different skills, and good readers need both.
exercise: «Decoding (turning letters into words) and comprehension (understanding what you read) are two different skills.» What does comprehension mean? | Turning letters into words | Writing a secret code | *Understanding what you read | Sounding out a long word
exercise: Sam sounds out c, a, t and says «cat». What is he doing? | Guessing the ending of a story | Counting the letters | *Decoding | Drawing a picture of a cat
exercise: In computing, what does decoding mean? | *Changing data into a form people or machines can use | Writing a poem | Deleting old files | Typing faster on a keyboard
test: When you blend the sounds c, a, t together, what word do you read? | cot | tac | *cat | act
test: Which of these is decoding in reading? | *Sounding out letters to work out a written word | Drawing a map | Remembering a birthday | Guessing the ending of a film
exam: Why is decoding not the same as comprehension? | They mean exactly the same thing | Comprehension is only used in computing | *Decoding turns letters into words, while comprehension means understanding what you read | Decoding is a kind of drawing
